WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.203
APARNA SINHA: This is one
of my favorite audiences

00:00:02.203 --> 00:00:04.890
I feel that at KubeCon,
you get a mix of folks that

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:06.600
are interested in the
high level and also

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:10.420
really want to know the details
and play with the technology.

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:13.740
So today, we have an agenda
that's tailored just for you.

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:14.760
I'm Aparna Sinha.

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:20.010
I lead product for Kubernetes,
GKE, and our Anthos product.

00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:25.230
Today, we're going to talk about
how you can use Google Cloud

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:28.650
technologies to accelerate
your journey towards enterprise

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:30.930
transformation.

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:33.330
Google Cloud has had
outstanding success

00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:37.800
in helping companies of all
sizes really modernize and move

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:39.570
to the cloud.

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:43.050
And every industry today
is the digital industry.

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:46.320
We're seeing technology
transform entire sectors

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:48.950
and change the basis
for competition.

00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:51.660
And I think we see it all
in our daily lives, too.

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:53.980
If you think about how
you got here today,

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:58.770
whether you use a taxi service,
how you educate your children,

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:00.870
how you buy your
groceries, all of that

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:04.260
has really changed in
the last five years.

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:08.010
On a personal note, my
children's school curriculum

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:10.080
actually runs on Google Cloud.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:13.380
And so I can say from experience
that Google Cloud is really

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:15.430
leading this transformation.

00:01:15.430 --> 00:01:17.980
And it's not just
for small companies.

00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:20.430
It's in companies that
are hundreds of years old

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:23.730
that are looking to
transform their industry.

00:01:23.730 --> 00:01:25.450
And how do we do this?

00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:27.930
We do this through
cutting edge technology.

00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:29.880
In Google Cloud, in
particular, technology

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:32.940
that enables applications
that are highly personal that

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:37.470
have intelligence that are agile
that are efficient and that

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:40.630
give you access.

00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:43.330
So if you're on
an IT team today,

00:01:43.330 --> 00:01:45.150
what does this mean for you?

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:47.790
I think many of you in the
audience already experienced

00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:48.760
this.

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:51.510
But when I look at the
role of the IT team

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:55.150
from five years ago to
today, it has really changed.

00:01:55.150 --> 00:01:59.460
It used to be that IT was
responsible for cost reduction.

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:01.830
And the funny thing
is that IT is still

00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:04.300
responsible for cost reduction.

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:08.039
But on top of that, you're also
responsible for contributing

00:02:08.039 --> 00:02:09.479
to the top line.

00:02:09.479 --> 00:02:12.420
You're responsible for
ensuring faster time

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:15.533
to market and agility.

00:02:15.533 --> 00:02:16.950
And that's not
easy because you've

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:18.990
got to do more with less.

00:02:18.990 --> 00:02:21.450
And you've got to make sure
that you get to that agility

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:25.000
without compromising security.

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:28.380
So when I talk to
CIOs today, they say,

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:31.690
in order to enable
true speed to market,

00:02:31.690 --> 00:02:34.020
I have to have an
agile platform.

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:36.420
And I have two questions
associated with that.

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:40.650
Number one, how do I
either build this platform

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:42.360
or get this platform?

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:45.300
Should I buy something off the
shelf that's enterprise ready?

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:46.860
Or should I build
it from scratch

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:49.350
and continuously maintain it?

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:52.740
And the second question is,
how do I balance my investment

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:56.340
in existing systems or systems
on-prem and modernizing

00:02:56.340 --> 00:02:59.880
those systems, versus what
I'm using in the cloud?

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:04.050
Those are the two most
pressing questions that I hear.

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:07.830
And actually, the answer to
these two questions is related.

00:03:07.830 --> 00:03:10.770
In our view, you've got
to pick a platform that

00:03:10.770 --> 00:03:14.910
is consistent that
actually runs both on-prem,

00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:18.370
as well as in public cloud,
and, in fact, in multiple public

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:19.210
clouds.

00:03:19.210 --> 00:03:22.660
That's the reality of today.

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:25.470
And so in choosing
this platform,

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:27.720
the reason that's
important is because it

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:30.910
allows you to maximize the
investment in your talent.

00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:33.960
So you don't have to retrain
your engineers in every cloud

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:36.520
environment.

00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:38.880
And ultimately, building
this platform yourself

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:41.160
is not a great idea,
because it takes

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:45.630
a lot of top talented engineers
to build such a thing.

00:03:45.630 --> 00:03:48.260
Those engineers could be
better utilized actually

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:51.680
contributing to business
differentiating capability.

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:56.570
This is why earlier this year,
Google Cloud introduced Anthos.

00:03:56.570 --> 00:04:00.110
Anthos is a
modernization platform.

00:04:00.110 --> 00:04:03.560
It's meant for you to develop
applications and run them

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:04.820
anywhere.

00:04:04.820 --> 00:04:07.380
And it's a platform
that's very flexible.

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:09.890
It can run in your
on-prem environment.

00:04:09.890 --> 00:04:11.540
It can run in Google Cloud.

00:04:11.540 --> 00:04:13.190
It can run in other clouds.

00:04:13.190 --> 00:04:17.149
And it can also run at the
edge in your branch locations,

00:04:17.149 --> 00:04:21.459
in retail stores,
even in stadiums.

00:04:21.459 --> 00:04:24.950
What's unique about
Anthos is three things.

00:04:24.950 --> 00:04:27.070
Number one, that
it's software-based.

00:04:27.070 --> 00:04:28.990
Most people that I
talked to already

00:04:28.990 --> 00:04:31.180
have quite a bit of hardware.

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:33.165
They've, perhaps,
chosen a hardware

00:04:33.165 --> 00:04:34.540
vendor that they're
working with.

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:36.610
They've got contracts in place.

00:04:36.610 --> 00:04:38.390
Or in some of the
edge locations,

00:04:38.390 --> 00:04:41.950
there's a lot of constraint in
terms of what the footprint is.

00:04:41.950 --> 00:04:43.630
So having a software
platform means

00:04:43.630 --> 00:04:45.190
that you have flexibility.

00:04:45.190 --> 00:04:47.710
You can purchase new
hardware if you choose to.

00:04:47.710 --> 00:04:51.790
Or you can try and reuse
what you already have.

00:04:51.790 --> 00:04:56.060
The second unique capability is
that it's built on open source.

00:04:56.060 --> 00:04:59.350
It's open, but it's opinionated.

00:04:59.350 --> 00:05:01.910
And the opinionation is
actually really important.

00:05:01.910 --> 00:05:04.240
It means that,
with Anthos, we're

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:08.350
building in all that Google has
learned about how to run an SRE

00:05:08.350 --> 00:05:11.810
function, how to manage
applications at scale, how

00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:13.330
to do container management.

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:15.760
That's part of the platform.

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:17.710
Open means that it's portable.

00:05:17.710 --> 00:05:19.930
But it's not so hard to manage.

00:05:19.930 --> 00:05:22.750
And then, lastly, it
abstracts the infrastructure

00:05:22.750 --> 00:05:27.902
so that your folks can actually
focus on business innovation.

00:05:27.902 --> 00:05:29.860
And that last part is
something that we learned

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:32.440
from our experience with GKE.

00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:36.220
I think many of the folks in
the crowd today have used GKE.

00:05:36.220 --> 00:05:39.460
Perhaps we can have a
show of hands how many?

00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:40.870
You've used GKE.

00:05:40.870 --> 00:05:42.430
Yeah, about 80% of the audience.

00:05:42.430 --> 00:05:44.800
That's quite good.

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:48.260
So GKE was introduced five
years ago, actually in November.

00:05:48.260 --> 00:05:51.710
We're celebrating the
fifth birthday of GKE.

00:05:51.710 --> 00:05:53.960
And it has seen
incredible success.

00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:56.960
You can see we've
got a lot of users

00:05:56.960 --> 00:06:00.770
across industries
and at various sizes.

00:06:00.770 --> 00:06:02.390
It's top-rated by analysts.

00:06:02.390 --> 00:06:04.520
And it's been
acknowledged by customers.

00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:06.110
I think the most
important thing here

00:06:06.110 --> 00:06:08.480
has been the success
that GKE has enabled

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:10.730
for its customers and
the type of applications

00:06:10.730 --> 00:06:12.260
that they've built.

00:06:12.260 --> 00:06:16.640
For example, eBay
has built a shop bot

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:20.510
that enables personalized
shopping on their mobile app.

00:06:20.510 --> 00:06:23.270
Pizza Hut enables
faster delivery

00:06:23.270 --> 00:06:26.990
of pizzas to you
during Super Bowl

00:06:26.990 --> 00:06:29.090
and also during any regular day.

00:06:29.090 --> 00:06:31.610
But these are things
that, as a product lead,

00:06:31.610 --> 00:06:36.740
it's really heartwarming to see
our customers implement changes

00:06:36.740 --> 00:06:39.810
that affect our lives every day.

00:06:39.810 --> 00:06:41.387
And why is that possible?

00:06:41.387 --> 00:06:43.220
That's possible, because
even though they're

00:06:43.220 --> 00:06:45.590
using Kubernetes,
they're not actually

00:06:45.590 --> 00:06:47.760
managing the Kubernetes.

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:50.150
GKE is taking away
a lot of the toil so

00:06:50.150 --> 00:06:53.150
that they can focus on building
new experiences for their end

00:06:53.150 --> 00:06:54.950
users.

00:06:54.950 --> 00:06:56.480
That's the key learning.

00:06:56.480 --> 00:06:57.690
How did we do this?

00:06:57.690 --> 00:07:00.890
We did this actually by
abstracting the infrastructure

00:07:00.890 --> 00:07:04.040
and automating more and more
capabilities over the last five

00:07:04.040 --> 00:07:04.880
years.

00:07:04.880 --> 00:07:07.970
We started by managing
the control plane

00:07:07.970 --> 00:07:11.810
and then providing auto
repair, auto scaling, and auto

00:07:11.810 --> 00:07:13.700
management of the nodes.

00:07:13.700 --> 00:07:16.070
And then we saw
that actually was

00:07:16.070 --> 00:07:18.030
really helpful to our users.

00:07:18.030 --> 00:07:20.570
So then we added
additional capabilities,

00:07:20.570 --> 00:07:22.940
like auto scaling of GPUs.

00:07:22.940 --> 00:07:25.400
And the result was that machine
learning workloads took off

00:07:25.400 --> 00:07:27.440
on GKE.

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:32.120
So as we add more automation,
we see more and more usage.

00:07:32.120 --> 00:07:34.410
We've now added
four-way autoscaling.

00:07:34.410 --> 00:07:37.170
So you can vertically
autoscale your pods.

00:07:37.170 --> 00:07:39.230
You can also
autoprovision, so you

00:07:39.230 --> 00:07:43.140
don't have to worry about the
efficiency of your cluster.

00:07:43.140 --> 00:07:44.870
It automatically takes
care to make sure

00:07:44.870 --> 00:07:47.892
that you're running in
the most efficient manner.

00:07:47.892 --> 00:07:49.100
And we're not stopping there.

00:07:49.100 --> 00:07:52.610
This list on the
left-hand side is just

00:07:52.610 --> 00:07:55.760
the number of new capabilities
added to GKE in the last two

00:07:55.760 --> 00:07:57.260
months.

00:07:57.260 --> 00:07:59.630
Here you see that we're
making the platform more

00:07:59.630 --> 00:08:02.930
secure through container
native networking.

00:08:02.930 --> 00:08:05.900
We're making it more
reliable, especially upgrades.

00:08:05.900 --> 00:08:08.480
I think earlier today someone
mentioned, hey, what are we

00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:10.040
doing to improve upgrades?

00:08:10.040 --> 00:08:14.340
We're making them more reliable
through a number of features--

00:08:14.340 --> 00:08:16.460
one of which is
called surge upgrades.

00:08:16.460 --> 00:08:18.320
Another capability
is release channels,

00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:22.250
which allows you to choose
your level of innovation-- how

00:08:22.250 --> 00:08:24.170
much of a cutting
edge release you

00:08:24.170 --> 00:08:26.780
want to use versus stability.

00:08:26.780 --> 00:08:29.060
And then in terms
of agility, we've

00:08:29.060 --> 00:08:31.370
been adding many more workloads.

00:08:31.370 --> 00:08:35.990
Today, 60% of workloads
on GKE are staple.

00:08:35.990 --> 00:08:39.298
That's databases
and batch workloads.

00:08:39.298 --> 00:08:40.840
And we're adding
support for Windows,

00:08:40.840 --> 00:08:46.520
which is moving to beta this
week, and also batch workloads.

00:08:46.520 --> 00:08:48.490
So what we learned
from this experience

00:08:48.490 --> 00:08:51.820
is that it's truly important
to give users choice.

00:08:51.820 --> 00:08:54.730
But that choice should come
with opinionation and management

00:08:54.730 --> 00:08:56.290
in order to be adoptable.

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:02.430
That's the foundation on
which Anthos is building.

00:09:02.430 --> 00:09:05.740
Anthos is taking that
GKE core and bringing it

00:09:05.740 --> 00:09:09.690
to the on-prem environment,
as well as to other clouds.

00:09:09.690 --> 00:09:12.570
But it's very much tailored
towards the enterprise

00:09:12.570 --> 00:09:14.550
and the CIO.

00:09:14.550 --> 00:09:18.000
We take the managed platform,
and we add several layers

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:19.710
on top.

00:09:19.710 --> 00:09:22.260
First of all, in an
enterprising environment,

00:09:22.260 --> 00:09:25.330
you need centralized policy
and configuration management.

00:09:25.330 --> 00:09:26.430
So that's a key element.

00:09:26.430 --> 00:09:29.070
It's at the heart of the
Anthos control plane.

00:09:29.070 --> 00:09:32.970
Secondly, with many
services across environments

00:09:32.970 --> 00:09:35.950
in your cloud, you need
service management.

00:09:35.950 --> 00:09:38.670
And that's something that comes
out of the box with Anthos.

00:09:38.670 --> 00:09:40.313
And then, most
importantly, in order

00:09:40.313 --> 00:09:41.730
to make the
developers productive,

00:09:41.730 --> 00:09:43.693
you need a seamless
developer experience.

00:09:43.693 --> 00:09:45.360
And that's something
that we're enabling

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:48.550
with Cloud Run on Anthos.

00:09:48.550 --> 00:09:51.690
So I'm going to try and
give you a whirlwind tour

00:09:51.690 --> 00:09:53.250
of the capabilities of Anthos.

00:09:53.250 --> 00:09:56.680
But the whole day is devoted to
going through these in detail.

00:09:56.680 --> 00:09:59.250
So this will just
be a quick one.

00:09:59.250 --> 00:10:02.610
Here, I'm going to cover five
unique aspects of the platform.

00:10:02.610 --> 00:10:04.500
First, how do you get started?

00:10:04.500 --> 00:10:06.180
Is it a forklift?

00:10:06.180 --> 00:10:08.220
Or is it easy to
get started with?

00:10:08.220 --> 00:10:10.800
Second, now that
you've started with it,

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:13.110
how do you make sure
that it's secure?

00:10:13.110 --> 00:10:16.820
Third, how can you make
those developers productive?

00:10:16.820 --> 00:10:19.960
Fourth, what about your
legacy applications?

00:10:19.960 --> 00:10:23.660
And then, lastly, how
do you operate at scale?

00:10:23.660 --> 00:10:25.230
So let's get into it.

00:10:25.230 --> 00:10:28.660
Anthos is not a rip
and replace platform.

00:10:28.660 --> 00:10:30.760
No matter where you
are in your journey,

00:10:30.760 --> 00:10:32.640
you can get started easily.

00:10:32.640 --> 00:10:37.060
And to enable that, we have
this capability so you can bring

00:10:37.060 --> 00:10:38.890
your own clusters to Anthos.

00:10:38.890 --> 00:10:41.080
And we're going to
demo that later today.

00:10:41.080 --> 00:10:42.220
But it's already in beta.

00:10:42.220 --> 00:10:44.630
And many customers are using it.

00:10:44.630 --> 00:10:46.870
What that means is that,
if you have a Kubernetes

00:10:46.870 --> 00:10:50.500
cluster somewhere-- and most
enterprises have Kubernetes

00:10:50.500 --> 00:10:51.820
somewhere at this point.

00:10:51.820 --> 00:10:54.280
If it's a conformant cluster,
you can connect it up

00:10:54.280 --> 00:10:56.260
to the Anthos control plane.

00:10:56.260 --> 00:10:58.060
And you can get a
single pane of glass

00:10:58.060 --> 00:11:01.600
for visibility, as well as
for workload management.

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:04.450
If you don't have a cluster,
as many of you know,

00:11:04.450 --> 00:11:06.070
it's easy to get
started with GKE.

00:11:06.070 --> 00:11:08.200
You can start a cluster
in less than 5 minutes.

00:11:08.200 --> 00:11:11.230
And then it's part of
the Anthos control plane.

00:11:11.230 --> 00:11:14.800
Or you can use GKE on-prem,
our Anthos on-prem offering,

00:11:14.800 --> 00:11:16.730
and bring that to the cloud.

00:11:16.730 --> 00:11:19.390
If you have no containerized
applications at all--

00:11:19.390 --> 00:11:21.190
we'll talk about later today--

00:11:21.190 --> 00:11:24.340
you can migrate existing
VM-based applications

00:11:24.340 --> 00:11:26.580
into Anthos as well.

00:11:26.580 --> 00:11:28.330
So there are many ways
to getting started.

00:11:28.330 --> 00:11:33.570
And it doesn't have to be a
lift and shift or a forklift.

00:11:33.570 --> 00:11:37.320
Second, once you get
started, how do you make sure

00:11:37.320 --> 00:11:39.590
that the environment is secure?

00:11:39.590 --> 00:11:42.440
Anthos has a number of
capabilities built in

00:11:42.440 --> 00:11:47.310
to ensure security by default.
There's actually a long list.

00:11:47.310 --> 00:11:50.145
But some of the ones that
I'd like to cover and show

00:11:50.145 --> 00:11:51.450
in this animated GIF--

00:11:54.520 --> 00:11:58.060
Anthos brings the ability
to have consistent security

00:11:58.060 --> 00:12:01.240
and configuration enabled
from a single source of truth.

00:12:01.240 --> 00:12:03.460
Typically this is a Git repo.

00:12:03.460 --> 00:12:05.350
You set your policies
in the Git repo.

00:12:05.350 --> 00:12:07.330
And these are policies
that could be security

00:12:07.330 --> 00:12:09.520
policies related to your mesh.

00:12:09.520 --> 00:12:12.910
They could be RBAC policies
related to Kubernetes or quota

00:12:12.910 --> 00:12:13.900
policies.

00:12:13.900 --> 00:12:15.860
And from the single
source of truth,

00:12:15.860 --> 00:12:19.360
these policies are distributed
to all of the enrolled clusters

00:12:19.360 --> 00:12:20.800
and then enforced.

00:12:20.800 --> 00:12:24.940
What this GIF shows is an
Anthos policy controller.

00:12:24.940 --> 00:12:28.060
This controller texts
all of the access

00:12:28.060 --> 00:12:31.390
to any API in your
cluster and make sure

00:12:31.390 --> 00:12:34.660
that it complies with
the enterprise policies

00:12:34.660 --> 00:12:36.790
that you've set.

00:12:36.790 --> 00:12:38.440
That's just one of the controls.

00:12:38.440 --> 00:12:41.980
There's also security controls
built into the service mesh.

00:12:41.980 --> 00:12:46.880
And then, lastly, Anthos comes
with binary authorization,

00:12:46.880 --> 00:12:51.040
which enables you to
define security checks

00:12:51.040 --> 00:12:53.170
upstream in the
development process,

00:12:53.170 --> 00:12:57.010
making sure that only validated
verified images are integrated

00:12:57.010 --> 00:12:59.980
into your building
release process.

00:12:59.980 --> 00:13:02.890
Security is not just
for security's sake.

00:13:02.890 --> 00:13:05.470
Security is what actually
enables development

00:13:05.470 --> 00:13:07.710
to go faster because it
doesn't stand in the way.

00:13:11.780 --> 00:13:14.470
So once you've made
a secure environment,

00:13:14.470 --> 00:13:16.970
and you've brought your
environment into Anthos,

00:13:16.970 --> 00:13:18.830
then comes the most
important task,

00:13:18.830 --> 00:13:22.370
which is building applications.

00:13:22.370 --> 00:13:24.080
So how do we make that easier?

00:13:24.080 --> 00:13:26.090
There's two ways.

00:13:26.090 --> 00:13:30.040
First, Anthos comes with
a Kubernetes application

00:13:30.040 --> 00:13:31.450
marketplace.

00:13:31.450 --> 00:13:34.750
That makes it really easy
to procure applications that

00:13:34.750 --> 00:13:38.650
are already tested and
available from both first party

00:13:38.650 --> 00:13:40.510
and third-party providers.

00:13:40.510 --> 00:13:42.645
This GIF is showing
you CloudBees.

00:13:42.645 --> 00:13:44.380
If you want to deploy
CloudBees, it's

00:13:44.380 --> 00:13:46.390
actually a single
click deployment

00:13:46.390 --> 00:13:48.340
from the Anthos marketplace.

00:13:48.340 --> 00:13:50.710
And you can choose which
cluster you want to deploy to.

00:13:50.710 --> 00:13:52.990
It can be any of the
clusters that are enrolled,

00:13:52.990 --> 00:13:56.470
including on-prem clusters
or clusters in other clouds.

00:13:56.470 --> 00:13:59.470
And this service is supported
by the partner-- in this case,

00:13:59.470 --> 00:14:01.810
CloudBees-- and comes
with integrated billing

00:14:01.810 --> 00:14:04.150
from Google Cloud.

00:14:04.150 --> 00:14:07.160
This is, of course, if you want
to reuse existing services.

00:14:07.160 --> 00:14:08.750
What about the most
important piece,

00:14:08.750 --> 00:14:11.620
which is your own services that
your developers will build?

00:14:11.620 --> 00:14:14.380
And that's where the easy
application development

00:14:14.380 --> 00:14:17.660
experience of Anthos
and Cloud Run comes in.

00:14:17.660 --> 00:14:22.960
So this is showing you how
you can take a container image

00:14:22.960 --> 00:14:26.530
and deploy it into
a cluster and have

00:14:26.530 --> 00:14:29.800
an internet-exposed application
in less than a minute.

00:14:29.800 --> 00:14:32.390
It's an extremely
simple user experience.

00:14:32.390 --> 00:14:34.900
It works for clusters
in Google Cloud.

00:14:34.900 --> 00:14:36.910
It also works now
for clusters on-prem.

00:14:41.770 --> 00:14:45.980
The fourth aspect-- you've got
your cloud native applications.

00:14:45.980 --> 00:14:48.970
But what about your
legacy applications?

00:14:48.970 --> 00:14:51.790
And that's where Migrate
for Anthos comes in.

00:14:51.790 --> 00:14:55.660
This is actually
brand new technology.

00:14:55.660 --> 00:14:57.160
It's quite differentiated.

00:14:57.160 --> 00:14:58.990
It doesn't exist anywhere else.

00:14:58.990 --> 00:15:03.040
And what it does is it takes
existing monolithic workloads

00:15:03.040 --> 00:15:04.360
that are residing in VMs.

00:15:04.360 --> 00:15:06.070
They could be in VMware VMs.

00:15:06.070 --> 00:15:08.500
Or they could be in
VMs in another cloud.

00:15:08.500 --> 00:15:12.520
And it automatically
migrates those applications

00:15:12.520 --> 00:15:15.850
into a StatefulSet in
Kubernetes and brings it

00:15:15.850 --> 00:15:18.890
into the Anthos environment.

00:15:18.890 --> 00:15:20.830
There are certain
benefits of doing this.

00:15:20.830 --> 00:15:22.150
It's not a magic solution.

00:15:22.150 --> 00:15:23.950
It doesn't get you
a microservices.

00:15:23.950 --> 00:15:25.580
It's still a monolith.

00:15:25.580 --> 00:15:27.580
But the benefits of
doing this is that one,

00:15:27.580 --> 00:15:29.510
it's a lot easier to get in.

00:15:29.510 --> 00:15:31.870
And secondly, it
actually does give you

00:15:31.870 --> 00:15:33.310
the advantages of bin packing.

00:15:33.310 --> 00:15:35.350
So you get a more
efficient environment

00:15:35.350 --> 00:15:38.230
where you're using your monolith
in a more effective way.

00:15:38.230 --> 00:15:41.020
Usually, the efficiency is
from such a migration actually

00:15:41.020 --> 00:15:42.530
pay for the migration itself.

00:15:45.260 --> 00:15:48.850
And finally, once you have
these applications into Anthos,

00:15:48.850 --> 00:15:50.990
how do you manage them at scale?

00:15:50.990 --> 00:15:52.450
That's the fifth part.

00:15:52.450 --> 00:15:56.110
And that is what we enable
with service management--

00:15:56.110 --> 00:15:58.670
Anthos Service Management.

00:15:58.670 --> 00:16:00.610
This is really built
upon the best practices

00:16:00.610 --> 00:16:05.990
that we found within Google
through our SRE function.

00:16:05.990 --> 00:16:09.340
The service graph essentially
is an at-a-glance view

00:16:09.340 --> 00:16:11.870
of all the services
in your environment.

00:16:11.870 --> 00:16:14.320
It gives you a
hierarchical picture

00:16:14.320 --> 00:16:16.185
where you can drill
down into the resources.

00:16:16.185 --> 00:16:17.560
And you can see
the relationships

00:16:17.560 --> 00:16:18.910
between the services.

00:16:18.910 --> 00:16:21.310
You can see the traffic
flow between the services

00:16:21.310 --> 00:16:26.420
and monitor various important
metrics, like the latency.

00:16:26.420 --> 00:16:29.280
And you can set SLOs
on those metrics

00:16:29.280 --> 00:16:33.630
to see when there are violations
and also policies to remediate.

00:16:33.630 --> 00:16:35.130
These are some of
the best practices

00:16:35.130 --> 00:16:37.470
that we have learned
through management

00:16:37.470 --> 00:16:40.170
of many microservices
in our environment.

00:16:40.170 --> 00:16:42.780
And we found that the
largest customers in GKE

00:16:42.780 --> 00:16:44.920
have, in fact,
many, many services.

00:16:44.920 --> 00:16:48.650
And this becomes a
necessity at that point.

00:16:48.650 --> 00:16:51.700
So those are the five kind
of core pillars of Anthos

00:16:51.700 --> 00:16:55.570
that we're going to walk
through in detail today.

00:16:55.570 --> 00:16:57.970
Each of the sessions
later today will go in

00:16:57.970 --> 00:17:01.680
and show you the
technology, show you

00:17:01.680 --> 00:17:05.410
what the capabilities are in
the form of an actual demo.

00:17:05.410 --> 00:17:07.839
And you'll have a chance
to ask the engineering

00:17:07.839 --> 00:17:10.927
team and the product team
questions afterwards.

00:17:14.060 --> 00:17:16.579
In order to implement
a transformation

00:17:16.579 --> 00:17:18.980
in an enterprise, we
found that it's not

00:17:18.980 --> 00:17:21.380
enough to just cater to
the needs of developers

00:17:21.380 --> 00:17:23.839
or to the needs of
the platform team.

00:17:23.839 --> 00:17:26.630
That's why Anthos has so
many different capabilities--

00:17:26.630 --> 00:17:29.840
something that caters to each
role in the organization.

00:17:29.840 --> 00:17:33.530
For the platform engineering
team, easy tools to help them

00:17:33.530 --> 00:17:35.390
manage the operations
environment.

00:17:35.390 --> 00:17:39.200
For the developers,
integration with their IDEs

00:17:39.200 --> 00:17:41.900
and the ability to choose
from the marketplace

00:17:41.900 --> 00:17:44.960
or build an application
in a serverless fashion.

00:17:44.960 --> 00:17:48.528
For service operators, all the
goodness of the SRE tooling.

00:17:48.528 --> 00:17:50.570
And then, of course, for
security and compliance,

00:17:50.570 --> 00:17:54.140
built-in capability.

00:17:54.140 --> 00:17:56.480
And we're finding that
our customers are finding

00:17:56.480 --> 00:17:58.470
a lot of value from this.

00:17:58.470 --> 00:18:00.860
Some of our financial
services customers

00:18:00.860 --> 00:18:04.040
are improving their
agility by 13x.

00:18:04.040 --> 00:18:09.110
We've also found in the most
traditional of industries,

00:18:09.110 --> 00:18:11.300
a company, like Airbus,
being able to use

00:18:11.300 --> 00:18:14.790
serverless technology in their
on-prem and cloud environment.

00:18:14.790 --> 00:18:19.380
This is truly
revolutionizing their work.

00:18:19.380 --> 00:18:21.900
And in order to enable
it, we've got an ecosystem

00:18:21.900 --> 00:18:24.150
of partners for Anthos.

00:18:24.150 --> 00:18:26.620
At different layers
in the marketplace,

00:18:26.620 --> 00:18:28.230
we have technology partners.

00:18:28.230 --> 00:18:30.840
Increasingly more and more
enterprise applications

00:18:30.840 --> 00:18:34.200
are being containerized and made
available in the marketplace

00:18:34.200 --> 00:18:36.960
for you to use directly.

00:18:36.960 --> 00:18:40.920
In terms of platform
partners, Cisco, HP, and Dell

00:18:40.920 --> 00:18:42.550
have provided reference
architectures.

00:18:42.550 --> 00:18:44.290
Should you want to
buy new hardware,

00:18:44.290 --> 00:18:46.380
you can couple that with Anthos.

00:18:46.380 --> 00:18:49.120
And then several of
our service partners--

00:18:49.120 --> 00:18:50.880
some of whom are
even here today--

00:18:50.880 --> 00:18:53.205
are available to assist
with the migration process.

00:18:58.230 --> 00:19:01.650
Anthos is really enabling
new business opportunities.

00:19:01.650 --> 00:19:04.320
You saw several examples today.

00:19:04.320 --> 00:19:08.040
In financial services, in
retail and health sciences,

00:19:08.040 --> 00:19:11.040
customers are using
these capabilities

00:19:11.040 --> 00:19:13.350
to bring new experiences.

00:19:13.350 --> 00:19:17.730
HSBC, for example, is enabling
a transformation in banking

00:19:17.730 --> 00:19:20.040
to reach their
customers more easily.

00:19:20.040 --> 00:19:22.590
In retail and commerce,
particularly with the edge,

00:19:22.590 --> 00:19:24.480
you're seeing much
more innovation

00:19:24.480 --> 00:19:27.150
happening with
the retail season.

00:19:27.150 --> 00:19:29.310
And then, lastly
in health care, we

00:19:29.310 --> 00:19:33.240
have companies that are using
AI to improve the drug discovery

00:19:33.240 --> 00:19:35.050
process.

00:19:35.050 --> 00:19:37.080
The question is really,
what will all of you

00:19:37.080 --> 00:19:38.560
do with this platform?

00:19:38.560 --> 00:19:41.310
And I think that is what
we hope to inspire today

00:19:41.310 --> 00:19:43.740
through the discussion.

00:19:43.740 --> 00:19:45.080
Thank you.

00:19:45.080 --> 00:19:48.430
[APPLAUSE]

